---

<BaseLayout
  title="60 vs 80 Mesh: Which Powder Grind to Choose | Tenda Peppers"
  description="60 mesh is about 250 µm and 80 mesh about 180 µm — both inside the standard 60–80 mesh powder range. What each grind means for dispersion and mouthfeel."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-23"
  dateModified="2026-09-23"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}

>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; 60 vs 80 Mesh
    </nav>

    <h1>60 vs 80 Mesh: Which Powder Grind to Choose</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      60 and 80 mesh are both inside the standard chilli powder range — the difference is particle size: about 250 µm versus about 180 µm. This comparison explains what the finer grind changes and how to choose.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Mesh is the number of openings per linear inch of a sieve — 80 mesh means 80 wires per inch and an opening of about 180 µm; 60 mesh is about 250 µm. Both sit inside the standard 60–80 mesh powder range: 60 is the standard powder grind, 80 is the finer, smoother grind.
    </div>
    <AtAGlance facts={glanceFacts} />

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">60 mesh</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">80 mesh</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Opening</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">≈250 µm</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">≈180 µm</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Wires per inch</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">60</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">80</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Character</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Fine — standard powder</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Fine — smooth powder</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Best for</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">General powder, seasoning blends</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Smoother powder, sauces</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Standard range</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">60–80 mesh for chilli &amp; paprika powder</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">60–80 mesh for chilli &amp; paprika powder</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the choice matters</h2>
    <p>
      Mesh is the particle-size scale: the number of openings per linear inch of a sieve. Higher mesh means finer particles — it measures the grind, not the heat or colour of the chilli. The full guide is on the <a href="/specs/mesh-size/">mesh size</a> page.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What the finer grind changes</h2>
    <p>
      Mesh controls three things at once: how evenly heat and colour disperse, how the powder feels on the palate, and how it suspends in a sauce. The 80-mesh grind disperses faster and feels smoother; the 60-mesh grind is the standard all-round powder. Coarser (20–40 mesh) is flakes; finer (100+ mesh) suits smooth sauces and instant mixes.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify</h2>
    <p>
      Name the mesh or the target particle size — it is adjustable to your requirement and documented in the quotation, alongside your SHU window and moisture.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat and colour values are representative. Confirm your exact SHU window, ASTA, form and mesh
      when you inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between 60 and 80 mesh?</h3>
      <p>60 mesh is about 250 µm and 80 mesh is about 180 µm — both are inside the standard 60–80 mesh powder range. The 80-mesh grind is finer and smoother.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which should I choose?</h3>
      <p>60–80 mesh is the standard powder range: 60 mesh for general powder and seasoning blends, 80 mesh for a smoother powder. Mesh is adjustable to your requirement — name the mesh or the target particle size.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does finer mesh change the heat?</h3>
      <p>No. Mesh measures the grind, not the heat or colour of the chilli. Finer mesh changes dispersion, mouthfeel and suspension — not SHU.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/specs/mesh-size/">Read the Mesh Guide</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/contact/" style="margin-left:8px;">Request a Quote</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
